# Line-ending policy.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: this repo is developed on Windows with
# `core.autocrlf=true`, and the deploy artifacts run on Linux. Without an
# explicit rule, `git add` rewrites shell scripts to CRLF on checkout and the
# VPS fails with the classic
#
#   /bin/bash^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# or, worse for a script that DOES run, `set -e` silently misparsing because a
# trailing `\r` became part of a value. A deploy script that cannot be trusted
# to be byte-identical across the checkout boundary is not a deploy script.
#
# `eol=lf` forces LF in the working tree regardless of `core.autocrlf`, so the
# bytes on the VPS equal the bytes here.

# Shell scripts and anything else executed directly on the VPS.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Container/config files that are parsed by tools with no CRLF tolerance.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
Dockerfile.* text eol=lf
*.dockerfile text eol=lf
Caddyfile text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.conf text eol=lf

# SOPS-encrypted payloads: `-text` = NO conversion in either direction, so the
# bytes are identical in the working tree and in the object store. This is
# deliberately stricter than `eol=lf`: the file is a cryptographic artifact
# (values + MAC), and a line-ending rewrite of a base64 blob is exactly the kind
# of "harmless" transformation that makes a secret undecryptable at 3am.
*.enc -text
